1
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,200
This is how infrastructure breaks. Quietly,
then all at once.

2
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:25,750
And now, AI writes the code.

3
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,950
Fewer people ever visit the docs, or the
sponsor button.

4
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,361
One click redirects to PayPal to approve a
monthly budget.

5
00:00:50,411 --> 00:00:53,550
That is the agent's mandate, and its ceiling.

6
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:58,652
Then, one hook line. Only package names and
depth leave the machine.

7
00:00:58,702 --> 00:00:59,950
Never source code.

8
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:05,221
At month end, the AI Treasurer answers typed
questions about each package.

9
00:01:05,271 --> 00:01:08,925
How critical is it? How much do its
maintainers need?

10
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:10,256
Same budget.

11
00:01:10,306 --> 00:01:16,900
The AI's judgment moves this package from
sixty-nine dollars to a hundred and sixty-one.

12
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:21,198
A security advisory? The AI can propose a
grant.

13
00:01:21,248 --> 00:01:24,325
Only a human can approve it, on PayPal.

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,560
A poisoned README tells the model to max out
its score. The answer is rejected.

15
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:37,625
Neutral weight.

16
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:42,650
A recipient changed their payout email
yesterday. Held for review.

17
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,500
Then seven rules, enforced in code, before a
single cent moves.

18
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:53,300
The AI decides where the money goes. The code
decides where it can never go.

19
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:00,837
Maya, a fictional maintainer, sees exactly why
she was paid.

20
00:02:00,887 --> 00:02:04,725
One sixty-one twenty-nine, plus the two-fifty
grant.

21
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:14,075
Run it twice. One batch. No double pay.

22
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,125
Both sides get a statement anyone can
recompute.
